-- RENDERING: Painting the Night Sky Background
-- This file handles the visual background layer: a dark gradient sky with twinkling stars.
-- None of this affects gameplay. It is purely aesthetic.


-- SECTION: Star State

-- VARIABLE: stars
-- TYPE: List of star objects
-- PURPOSE: Holds all the background stars. Each star has x, y, size, and brightness.
-- The list is created once and updated every frame to make stars twinkle.


-- SECTION: Initializing Stars

-- FUNCTION: initStars
-- PARAMETERS: canvasWidth, canvasHeight
-- PURPOSE: Creates 100 randomly placed stars across the entire canvas.
-- Called once when the game starts or the canvas is resized.
--
-- set stars to an empty list.
-- count to 100 and when you are done create one star:
--   set x to a random number between 0 and the canvas width.
--   set y to a random number between 0 and the canvas height.
--   set size to a random number between 0.5 and 2.5 pixels.
--   -- Small size range creates a mix of dim distant stars and brighter close ones.
--   set brightness to a random number between 0 and 1.
--   -- Brightness controls opacity. 0 is invisible, 1 is fully bright.
--
-- EDUCATIONAL NOTE: Using random values for position, size, and brightness
-- ensures no two game sessions have identical star fields.
-- This technique is called "procedural generation" - creating content with algorithms
-- instead of hand-placing every element.


-- SECTION: Updating Stars (Twinkling)

-- FUNCTION: updateStars
-- PURPOSE: Makes stars flicker slightly each frame to simulate twinkling.
-- Called every frame during the game loop.
--
-- FOR EACH star in the stars list:
--   Add a small random value between -0.05 and 0.05 to the star's brightness.
--   -- This creates subtle random fluctuation.
--   Clamp brightness to stay between 0.3 and 1.
--   -- CONDITIONAL: if brightness is less than 0.3 then set brightness to 0.3.
--   -- CONDITIONAL: if brightness is greater than 1 then set brightness to 1.
--   -- The minimum of 0.3 ensures stars never fully disappear.
--   -- The maximum of 1 prevents brightness from exceeding full opacity.
--
-- EDUCATIONAL NOTE: "Clamping" means forcing a value to stay within a range.
-- It is used constantly in games to prevent values from going out of bounds.
-- The formula is: value = max(minimum, min(maximum, value))


-- SECTION: Drawing the Background

-- FUNCTION: drawBackground
-- PARAMETERS: ctx (canvas rendering context), canvasWidth, canvasHeight
-- PURPOSE: Paints the gradient sky and all stars. Called every frame before other elements.
--
-- STEP 1: Create a vertical gradient (dark sky).
--   The gradient goes from top to bottom with three color stops:
--     0 percent (top):    "#0a0015" - Very dark purple-black (deep space).
--     50 percent (middle): "#150520" - Slightly lighter purple (atmospheric glow).
--     100 percent (bottom): "#1a0a10" - Dark reddish-brown (ground glow from fire below).
--   Fill the entire canvas with this gradient.
--   -- EDUCATIONAL NOTE: A "gradient" smoothly transitions between colors.
--   -- The three stops create a sky that feels like a dark volcanic landscape.
--   -- The reddish tint at the bottom hints at the fire hazards below.
--
-- STEP 2: Draw each star.
--   FOR EACH star in the stars list:
--     Set fill color to white with alpha equal to brightness times 0.8.
--     -- Multiplying by 0.8 caps the brightest star at 80 percent opacity.
--     -- This keeps stars subtle and prevents them from overpowering the scene.
--     Draw a filled circle (arc) at the star's position with its size as the radius.
--
-- EDUCATIONAL NOTE: The background is drawn FIRST every frame.
-- Then platforms, fire, coins, player, and UI are drawn ON TOP of it.
-- This layering order is called the "draw order" or "z-order".
-- If the background were drawn last, it would cover everything else.
